(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-cp-exam-2-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What underlies aneurysm and dissection at the level of the wall?","choices":["Excess elastin deposition stiffening the wall","Lipid accumulation beneath the endothelium","Bacterial colonization of the intima","Compromised wall connective tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Arterial walls are constantly remodelling, so any compromise in that connective tissue can produce either lesion, with histopathological change following. Either its structure or its function. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What does the raised left atrial pressure of mitral stenosis first lead to?","choices":["Left ventricular hypertrophy, then left sided failure","Aortic root dilation","Atrial enlargement and hypertrophy","Pericardial effusion and tamponade"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The increased pressure load on the left atrium leads to atrial chamber enlargement and hypertrophy. Only later may pulmonary vascular pressures rise, progressing to chronic pulmonary hypertension and right sided hypertrophy and failure. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Who is the typical patient with a primary spontaneous pneumothorax?","choices":["An older adult with emphysema","A tall, thin man aged 20–40","A trauma patient with a chest wound","A child with cystic fibrosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Primary spontaneous pneumothorax occurs in tall, thin men aged 20–40 with no underlying disease; cigarette smoking increases the risk. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia is a malignancy of which cells?","choices":["Mature B cells","Germinal-center B cells","Precursor B or T cells","Differentiated plasma cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia/lymphoma is a malignancy of precursor B or T cells (lymphoblasts): B-ALL/LBL from the B-cell precursor in the marrow, T-ALL/LBL from the T-cell precursor in the thymus. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 23, 28"},{"q":"What properties make homocysteine harmful to vessels?","choices":["It is atherogenic but protects against thrombosis","It is atherogenic and prothrombotic","It raises low density lipoprotein production in the liver","It inhibits smooth muscle proliferation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two harms at once: it damages the wall and it promotes clot on that damaged wall, which is the same one-two that drives coronary events generally. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why can airflow testing be normal in a person with asthma?","choices":["The obstruction is irreversible","The alveoli are destroyed","Airway inflammation is absent","Symptoms fluctuate between episodes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asthma symptoms fluctuate and testing may normalize between episodes, so the history is read alongside objective airflow measurements. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 35, 44"},{"q":"Why is IgM especially effective at agglutination?","choices":["It crosses the placenta","It has many binding sites","It binds mast-cell receptors","It has a secretory component"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Secreted IgM is usually a pentamer that places many binding sites around one molecule, so it links antigen-bearing particles into clumps; agglutination is one of its main functions. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 45, 46, 47"},{"q":"Why does impaired helper T-cell function weaken antibody responses?","choices":["Helper T cells secrete the antibodies","Helper signals drive B-cell differentiation","Helper T cells form the B-cell receptor","Helper T cells carry antibody to tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Helper signals support B-cell differentiation and antibody production, so losing them weakens the antibody response even though plasma cells do the secreting. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin binds mast cells and basophils?","choices":["IgE","IgD","IgA","IgM"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. IgE binds mast cells and basophils through high-affinity receptors and contributes to allergy and parasite defense. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 44, 46"},{"q":"Which cell, when seen on a stained smear, is NOT a normal constituent of peripheral blood?","choices":["Monocyte","Small lymphocyte","Eosinophil","Plasma cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A plasma cell is not a normal constituent of peripheral blood; it is shown beside the nongranular leukocytes for comparison. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How are the coronary syndromes classified?","choices":["By which coronary artery is involved","By the severity and onset of cardiac symptoms","By the lipoprotein pattern of the patient","By the degree of luminal occlusion alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The division is clinical rather than anatomical, which is why two syndromes with similar plaques sit in different categories. Into chronic and acute. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How do natural killer cells differ from cytotoxic T cells?","choices":["They mature in the thymus","They secrete antibodies","They need a prior exposure first","They need no specific antigen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unlike cytotoxic T cells, natural killer cells do not need to recognize a specific antigen; they target tumor cells and protect against many infectious microbes. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why does slowly progressive plaque formation allow the heart to tolerate more narrowing?","choices":["The myocardium reduces its oxygen demand permanently","The plaque itself becomes permeable to blood","Collateral circulation develops over the years the plaque is growing","Cardiac cells switch to anaerobic metabolism indefinitely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Time is what permits the adaptation, which is also why an abrupt occlusion in a previously healthy vessel can do more damage than a severe chronic stenosis. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which lipoprotein carries the highest risk of atherosclerosis?","choices":["Low density lipoprotein, rich in cholesterol","High density lipoprotein, rich in cholesterol","Very low density lipoprotein, high in cholesterol","Lipoproteins carry equal risk whatever their content"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low density lipoprotein is high in cholesterol and carries the highest risk of atherosclerosis. Very low density lipoprotein, high in triglycerides, also increases risk, while high density lipoprotein decreases it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why does a high afterload worsen mitral regurgitation?","choices":["It increases the amount of blood regurgitated","It reduces left atrial pressure","It prevents the ventricle from dilating","It converts the regurgitation into stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The ventricle takes the path of least resistance, so the harder it is to eject forwards, the more goes backwards. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which absolute neutrophil count defines severe neutropenia?","choices":["Below 200 cells/µL","Below 500 cells/µL","500 to 1,000 cells/µL","1,000 to 1,500 cells/µL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Neutropenia is graded by absolute neutrophil count: mild 1,000 to 1,500, moderate 500 to 1,000, severe below 500 cells/µL, and agranulocytosis below 200. Severe neutropenia increases the risk of serious infection. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"In V(D)J recombination, which gene segments do light chains use?","choices":["V, D and J","V and J","V and D","D and J"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Developing B cells rearrange their immunoglobulin genes in the marrow: heavy chains use V, D and J segments, while light chains use only V and J. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does the secondary response differ from the primary response?","choices":["Faster and stronger, often IgG","Slower, and dominated by IgM","Same speed but with more IgE","It needs no memory cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Memory cells support a generally faster and stronger response on re-exposure, and class-switched antibodies, often IgG, may reach higher levels and persist longer. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 36, 37"},{"q":"What distinguishes hypoxia from hypoxemia?","choices":["Hypoxia is low arterial oxygen","Hypoxia is high arterial carbon dioxide","Hypoxia is low tissue oxygenation","Hypoxia is low hemoglobin saturation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxia is decreased tissue oxygenation; hypoxemia is decreased blood oxygen, seen as a low arterial oxygen and low hemoglobin saturation. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the structure of an atheroma?","choices":["A calcified nodule within the media","A soft lipid core with a fibrous cap","A blood-filled channel within the wall","A thin-walled outpouching of the vessel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An atheroma is an intimal lesion with a soft lipid core under a fibrous cap, projecting into the lumen of the vessel. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How does deep vein thrombosis lead to pulmonary embolus?","choices":["The thrombus propagates continuously up the venous system","Fibrosis of the vein raises pulmonary pressure directly","The clot crosses into the arterial circulation at the capillaries","The thrombus dislodges and travels to the pulmonary artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, moving freely through the venous system until it reaches the pulmonary artery and occludes it &mdash; an arterial occlusion produced by a venous disease. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which function of immunity removes cellular debris?","choices":["Defense","Surveillance","Memory","Homeostasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Immunity is a state of protection with three functions: defense (resistance to invaders), surveillance (of self for mutant or cancerous cells) and homeostasis (removing cellular debris). — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How does the small vessel group subdivide?","choices":["ANCA-associated, and immune complex","Hyaline and hyperplastic","Saccular and fusiform","Superficial and deep"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with systemic lupus erythematosus and rheumatoid arthritis given as the immune complex examples. The first is a necrotising vasculitis. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which mechanism produces a transudative pleural effusion?","choices":["Increased permeability of inflamed pleura","Raised hydrostatic or lowered oncotic pressure","Impaired lymphatic drainage","Leakage of chyle from the thoracic duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Transudates are caused by increased hydrostatic or decreased oncotic pressure, as in severe heart failure, cirrhosis with ascites, nephrotic syndrome and myxedema. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 93, 94, 95"},{"q":"How does valvular stenosis change the work the heart does?","choices":["It imposes extra VOLUME work, since blood returns across the valve","It imposes extra PRESSURE work","It reduces the work of the heart by limiting flow","It has no hemodynamic effect until the valve is completely closed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is the distinction that organizes the whole valve section, and it predicts which chamber hypertrophies. Since blood must be forced through a smaller opening. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which mechanism defines type II hypersensitivity?","choices":["IgG or IgM binding cell or tissue antigens","IgE cross-linked on mast cells","Circulating complexes depositing in vessels","Sensitized T cells releasing cytokines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In type II hypersensitivity IgG or IgM binds antigens on cells or within tissues, and injury follows through phagocytosis, complement or antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What prevents gravitational reverse flow in veins?","choices":["Venous valves","A thick muscular media","Active pumping by the endothelium","Constriction of the adventitia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and their insufficiency is precisely what produces varicose veins, typically in the lower extremities where blood flows against gravity. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which complement fragment coats targets as an opsonin?","choices":["C5a","C3a","C3b","C9"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. C3b coats the target for opsonization by macrophages, while C3a, C4a and C5a promote the inflammatory response. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which three morphologic features give etiologic clues in anemia?","choices":["Size, shape and nuclear lobes","Color, count and granules","Volume, lifespan and removal site","Size, hemoglobinization and shape"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The morphologic characteristics that provide etiologic clues are red-cell size (normocytic, microcytic or macrocytic), degree of hemoglobinization seen as color (normochromic or hypochromic), and shape. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which disorder is classified as obstruction of the airway lumen?","choices":["Emphysema","Cystic fibrosis","Asthma","Chronic bronchitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The obstructive diseases are grouped by site; obstruction of the airway lumen lists bronchiectasis, bronchiolitis, cystic fibrosis, acute tracheobronchial obstruction, epiglottitis, croup and foreign body. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 33, 34"},{"q":"How can narrowing of a vessel lumen arise?","choices":["Only progressively over years","Only precipitously, as a sudden event","Progressively or precipitously","Only as a consequence of external compression"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the distinction matters because a gradual stenosis allows collateral supply to develop while a sudden one does not. Atherosclerosis narrows progressively; thrombosis or embolism precipitously. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is anemia primarily defined by?","choices":["Hemoglobin below the cutoff for the person","Hematocrit below the laboratory's interval","A red-cell count below the reference range","A mean cell volume outside 80 to 100 fL"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anemia reduces the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity, and it is defined primarily by hemoglobin below the cutoff for age, sex and pregnancy status; hematocrit only supports. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What influences stroke volume, and therefore part of cardiac output?","choices":["The diameter of the arterioles","The number of capillaries in the tissue","The elasticity of the adventitia","Blood volume"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stroke volume is influenced by blood volume, and blood volume is regulated by sodium excretion or resorption. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which bronchiectasis shape combines saccular and cylindrical features?","choices":["Saccular","Cylindrical","Centriacinar","Fusiform"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bronchiectasis is classified by bronchial shape: saccular (cavity-like), cylindrical, and fusiform (varicose), which combines saccular and cylindrical. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 62, 63"},{"q":"What does persistent alveolitis lead to in diffuse interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Obliterated capillaries and irreversible fibrosis","Bullae from trapped air","Permanent bronchial dilation","Return of normal alveolar walls"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Persistent alveolitis leads to obliteration of alveolar capillaries, reorganization of lung parenchyma and irreversible fibrosis, with large air-filled cysts. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is the role of the arterioles?","choices":["Exchange of fluid, nutrients and wastes with the tissues","Transport of blood back toward the heart","Collection of blood emerging from the capillary bed","Control conduits for release of blood to the capillaries"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is the same property that makes them the regulators of vascular resistance, which is one of the two determinants of blood pressure. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Approximately what is the normal mean pulmonary arterial pressure in a healthy adult?","choices":["8 mmHg","15 mmHg","30 mmHg","45 mmHg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normal pulmonary arterial pressure is about 22/8 to 25/8 mmHg, with a mean of 15 mmHg. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A pleural fluid meets only the lactate dehydrogenase ratio criterion for an exudate. How is it classified?","choices":["Transudate","Indeterminate until two criteria are met","Chylothorax","Exudate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One positive criterion supports exudate classification; a pleural to serum lactate dehydrogenase ratio above 0.6 is an exudate criterion even when the protein ratio is below 0.5. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 94, 95, 98"},{"q":"Which leukocyte normally makes up the largest share of the total white cell count?","choices":["Lymphocytes","Monocytes","Eosinophils","Neutrophils"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are normally 40 to 60 percent of the total leukocyte count, the largest share, ahead of lymphocytes at 20 to 40 percent. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which are given as causes of the initiating endothelial injury?","choices":["Valvular calcification and rheumatic scarring","Viral infection of the myocardium","Wall stress, toxins, inflammation, hyperlipidemia","Pericardial inflammation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mechanical, chemical, inflammatory and metabolic insults all converge on the same first step, which is why so many different risk factors end in the same lesion. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What defines coronary heart disease?","choices":["Insufficient delivery of oxygenated blood to the myocardium because of atherosclerotic vessels","Failure of the myocardium to contract because of intrinsic muscle disease","Inflammation of the coronary arteries with leukocyte infiltration","Obstruction of venous drainage from the myocardium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The definition is about supply failing rather than about pain or any particular syndrome, which is why so many different presentations sit underneath it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Helper T cells recognize antigenic peptides presented on which molecules?","choices":["Major histocompatibility complex class I","Surface immunoglobulin on B cells","Major histocompatibility complex class II","CD16 on natural killer cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Helper (CD4) T cells recognize antigenic peptides presented by major histocompatibility complex class II molecules. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which description fits the normal pulmonary circulation?","choices":["High flow, low pressure","Low flow, high pressure","High flow, high pressure","Low flow, low pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pulmonary circulation is normally a high-flow, low-pressure system: about 22/8 to 25/8 mmHg, mean 15 mmHg. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why does aortic stenosis predispose the heart to ischemia and angina?","choices":["Hypertrophy and high ventricular pressures raise demand beyond what supply can meet","The stenotic valve obstructs the coronary ostia directly","The ventricle dilates and thins, reducing perfusion","Calcium emboli lodge in the coronary arteries"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angina can arise with entirely normal coronary arteries, because the problem is on the demand side of the same equation. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which vessels does atherosclerosis principally affect?","choices":["Large elastic arteries such as the aorta","Medium-sized muscular arteries","Small arteries and arterioles within tissues","Capillaries"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct &mdash; the smaller branches of the aorta such as the coronary and renal arteries, roughly 3 to 4 mm. They combine elasticity with musculature to withstand high pulsatile forces and recoil to promote flow. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What happens to the vessel wall in fibromuscular dysplasia?","choices":["Segments are focally thickened by hyperplasia and fibrosis","The wall thins uniformly along its whole length","Lipid-laden macrophages accumulate beneath the endothelium","Inflammatory cells destroy the wall architecture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, in the media and intima layers, causing luminal stenosis. The exact cause is unknown, and medium and large muscular arteries are affected. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What pattern of chamber involvement can occur in myocarditis?","choices":["Isolated right ventricular hypertrophy","Left atrial enlargement alone","No chamber change, with inflammation confined to the endocardium","Left ventricular dysfunction, sometimes with chamber dilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Myocarditis is characterized by left ventricular dysfunction with general dilation of all four chambers, as the inflamed, edematous myocardium develops patchy or diffuse necrotic lesions and leukocyte infiltrates. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is usually secreted as a pentamer?","choices":["IgA","IgM","IgG","IgD"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. IgM is usually secreted as a pentamer with a J chain, and is a monomer on the B-cell surface. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 44, 45"},{"q":"Which inflammatory marker appears among the risk factors?","choices":["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Procalcitonin","C-reactive protein","Ferritin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, listed under inflammation alongside hyperlipidemia, lipoprotein a, smoking, hypertension, diabetes mellitus and metabolic syndrome. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"In hereditary spherocytosis, why are the red cells destroyed early?","choices":["Antibodies lyse the cells within the circulation","Their hemoglobin synthesis fails in the marrow","Rigid spherocytes are trapped and eaten in the spleen","The marrow releases them before they mature"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Biconcave young cells lose lipid membrane on repeated passage through the spleen and circulation, become rigid, inflexible spherocytes, are trapped in the spleen and destroyed prematurely by splenic macrophages: extravascular hemolysis. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which process defines the myeloproliferative neoplasms?","choices":["Ineffective hematopoiesis with dysplasia","Impaired differentiation with blast growth","Clonal proliferation of myeloid cells","Clonal growth of antibody-making B cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Myeloproliferative neoplasms are a clonal proliferation of myeloid cells: chronic myeloid leukemia, polycythemia vera, essential thrombocythemia and primary myelofibrosis. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which factors make up the common pathway?","choices":["XII, XI, IX and VIII","X, V, II and I","VII and tissue factor","VIII, IX and XIII"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The common pathway is factors X, V, II and I, which generate thrombin and fibrin; both the PT (prothrombin time) and the aPTT (activated partial thromboplastin time) assess it. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the primary pathogenic defect in acute leukemia?","choices":["Ineffective hematopoiesis","Premature red-cell destruction","Maturation arrest","Reactive bone marrow stimulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The primary pathogenic defect in acute leukemia is maturation arrest: acquired mutations in transcription factors block differentiation, and other mutations let the cells proliferate independently of growth factors. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which leukocyte binds IgE (immunoglobulin E)?","choices":["Neutrophil","Monocyte","Basophil","Natural killer cell"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Basophils bind IgE (immunoglobulin E) and take part in the parasite response and allergy; few should be seen on a normal complete blood count, so a rise should prompt thought of malignancy. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What are smooth muscle cells responsible for in response to stimuli?","choices":["Vasoconstriction or vasodilation","Phagocytosis of lipid","Production of nitric oxide to relax the vessel","Maintaining the nonthrombogenic surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they also proliferate when appropriately stimulated, which is the behavior that turns a repair response into a stenotic lesion. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What happens in immune heparin-induced thrombocytopenia?","choices":["Bleeding from the falling platelet count","Reduced marrow production of platelets","A clonal rise in the platelet count","Thrombosis despite a falling platelet count"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In immune heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, platelet activation causes thrombosis despite a falling count, so a low count does not mean a bleeding tendency here. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does immunofixation add to serum protein electrophoresis?","choices":["The heavy- and light-chain types","Separation of albumin from globulins","A measure of complement activity","A count of CD4 helper T cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Electrophoresis separates serum proteins into albumin and globulin fractions by migration in an electric field; immunofixation characterizes the immunoglobulin heavy- and light-chain types. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How does constrictive pericarditis impair the heart?","choices":["The sac fills rapidly with fluid, compressing the chambers","The heart adheres to surrounding mediastinal structures","A dense, scarred sac encases the heart, impairing filling","The myocardium becomes fibrotic and non-compliant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pericardial sac becomes dense, nonelastic, fibrous and scarred, encasing the heart like a stiff cage that impairs diastolic filling. The stiffness lies outside the muscle, unlike the fibrotic myocardium of restrictive cardiomyopathy. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What does the term “blue bloater” reflect in chronic bronchitis?","choices":["Alveolar destruction without fibrosis","Air trapped in distended bullae","Cyanosis and edema of right heart failure","Reversible bronchospasm with wheeze"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. “Blue bloater” reflects oxygen desaturation (cyanosis) and the edema associated with right-sided heart failure. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 48, 50"},{"q":"Which finding confirms a restrictive pattern?","choices":["Low forced vital capacity alone","Low FEV1/FVC ratio","High residual volume","Low total lung capacity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A low forced vital capacity alone does not establish restriction; a low total lung capacity confirms the restrictive pattern. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 59, 82"}]};
